# .secretlintignore
# Hand-maintained ignore patterns for secretlint.

# Binary/media file extensions
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.webp
*.svg
*.ico
*.woff
*.woff2
*.ttf
*.eot
*.lock
*.snap
*.pdf
*.zip
*.gz
*.tar

# repo-specific
pnpm-lock.yaml
# uv.lock removed -- already matched by *.lock above.
.env.example
# Not needed for the CI secretlint job itself -- secretlint already ignores
# any `.secretlintignore*` file by default. Listed here anyway because
# scan-staged-files.sh/guarded-dispatch.mjs's new .secretlintignore
# consultation (issue #295) has no knowledge of secretlint's own internal
# default-ignore list; without this entry, staging a real edit to this file
# still gets it flagged locally by its own filename (`*secret*`).
.secretlintignore
# Anchored to repo root (leading /) -- an unanchored bare name matches at any
# depth in gitignore syntax, which previously also exempted
# plugins/analysis-kit/tests/ and plugins/session-kit/tests/ from scanning,
# not just these top-level directories (issue #295).
/.agents
/.claude
/.codex
/.devin
/tests
# github-issue-creator drafts local issue reports here (freeform filenames
# derived from the issue's own short description). A draft that legitimately
# discusses secret-scanning tooling by name (e.g. a filename containing
# "secretlintignore", "credential", or "token") hits the same filename-only
# false positive as the curated single-file entries above, but the offending
# substrings here are arbitrary human-language words in a freeform filename,
# not an enumerable convention like evals/**/session-transcript-*.md below --
# no narrower pattern can cover this class without also being unbounded.
# Verified via a real `secretlint --format json` run (issue #309) against all
# 117 files present under issues/ at the time of this change: zero produced a
# real content-scan finding. Disclosed tradeoff, consistent with the other
# anchored directories above: this also removes issues/ from CI's real
# secretlint content scan, not just this repo's local filename check -- a
# real credential pasted into a future issue draft's reproduction/error-log
# section (this directory is explicitly meant to capture "messy input" per
# github-issue-creator's own description) would not be caught by either
# layer. Accepted the same way the existing anchored directories already
# accept it, rather than narrowed further, since no narrower filename pattern
# is available here.
/issues
# Verified via a real `secretlint --format json` run against every file that
# was previously listed here (63 total, issue #295): only the two entries
# below actually trigger a finding (fake Slack tokens / a fake Postgres
# connection string used as documentation/test fixtures). The other three
# don't trip secretlint's content scan, but their filename matches the same
# */secret/*|*credential*/ pattern plugins/git-kit/scripts/scan-staged-files.sh
# and codex-kit's guarded-dispatch.mjs use (via the shared
# plugins/codex-kit/scripts/lib/secret-filenames.mjs pattern list) -- kept so
# a future integration making those two tools consult this file doesn't
# regress on them. The remaining 58 originally-listed files matched neither
# check and were removed.
plugins/plugin-devkit/skills/skill-development/references/secrets-and-credentials.md
plugins/analysis-kit/scripts/redact_secrets.py
plugins/codex-kit/scripts/lib/secret-filenames.mjs
plugins/codex-kit/scripts/smoke-tests/codex-exec-secret-redaction.mjs
plugins/codex-kit/scripts/smoke-tests/git-untracked-secret-screen.mjs
# Verified via a real `secretlint --format json` run (issue #309) against a
# reproduction fixture matching this pattern
# (evals/analyzing-security-and-privacy/workspace/iteration-1/eval-1/
# session-transcript-credential.md): a fake Slack-token-shaped string
# (the issue's own AKIA-prefixed AWS-key example turned out not to trigger a
# finding -- it matches secretlint's own known-placeholder allowlist) produced
# a real SLACK_TOKEN finding without this entry, and none with it. Scoped to
# the `session-transcript-*` filename convention skill-tester eval fixtures
# use for this purpose, not a blanket /evals exemption -- evals.json and any
# other hand-authored skill content outside a workspace/ run directory stays
# scanned normally. The `workspace/` segment is required in the pattern
# itself, not just this comment (Codex finding, PR #311): an earlier version
# read `evals/**/session-transcript-*.md`, which `**`'s zero-or-more-segments
# semantics made match a hand-authored `evals/<skill>/session-transcript-*.md`
# with no `workspace/` in its path at all, contradicting this exact claim --
# verified live via `git check-ignore --no-index` both before (matched,
# wrongly) and after (correctly excluded) this fix.
evals/*/workspace/**/session-transcript-*.md

# .gitignore (repo root)
# node_modules, npm-debug.log, .venv, __pycache__, *.pyc, .pytest_cache,
# .ruff_cache, .ty, dist, build, reports/events, *.local/*.local.*/
# **/*.local.*, **/.draft, **/.backup, **/.temp, **/.experiments, and
# **/.claude-plugin/*.lock were removed from this list (issue #295):
# all are genuinely gitignored (never appear as a "changed" file unless
# force-added), and *.local/npm-debug.log specifically are the same class
# of risk .env already was -- a force-added file that could carry a real
# credential -- so exempting them here would silently reopen the same gap
# the .env removal below already closed (Codex finding, round 2).
# .env / .env.* deliberately NOT mirrored from .gitignore above: those
# patterns exist to keep untracked env files out of git, but secretlint
# only ever scans files the changed-files step already reports as
# tracked/changed -- a force-added (`git add -f`) .env file with a real
# credential would otherwise sail through this scan with a false-clean
# result (Codex finding, round 2).
.github/instructions/codacy.instructions.md
